course: dictionary
lesson: 7965
title: Breaking a word into parts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Long words are often made of smaller parts you already know. Rechargeable is built from re- (again), charge (fill up with electricity) and -able (able to be). Put the parts together and you get: able to be filled up with electricity again.
explain: «A rechargeable battery, like the one in your phone, can be pushed back.» The clue pushed back and the start re- both point to again. The charger moves the tiny particles back to where they started, so the battery is ready to go again.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into a sentence. «A rechargeable torch» can be filled with electricity again and used many times, so the meaning fits. Here charge means fill with electricity, not the price you pay.
fact: Re- at the start of a word often means again, as in refill, reuse and rewrite.
fact: The ending -able means able to be, so washable means able to be washed.
fact: «Rechargeable» means able to be filled up with electricity again, so it can be used many times.
fact: Rechargeable batteries are found in phones, tablets, toothbrushes and toys.
fact: Rechargeable batteries create less waste than single-use batteries, which is why they help the planet.
exercise: In rechargeable, what does re- mean? | Not | *Again | Before | Under
exercise: In rechargeable, what does the ending -able mean? | Full of | *Able to be | Without | Like a child
exercise: Which item most likely uses a rechargeable battery? | *A phone | A bowl of soup | A wooden spoon | A paper cup
test: «A rechargeable battery» can be pushed back. What does pushed back mean here? | Thrown away in the bin | Made into a toy | Sent to the shop | *Filled with electricity again
test: Why is a rechargeable battery better for the planet than a single-use one? | It is cheaper to buy in a shop | It lasts forever without charging | It is shiny | *It creates less waste
exam: Which rule is safe for a rechargeable battery? | Charge it near water and leave it there all night | *Only use the right charger | Put the battery in a fire to warm it | Charge a damaged battery to be sure
